<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Courland</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kurzeme</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Courland is a historical region of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, lying between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-riga">Gulf of Riga</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> and including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liepaja">Liepāja</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ventspils">Ventspils</a>. Conquered by the Livonian Order in the thirteenth century, it became the Duchy of Courland and Semigallia under Polish-Lithuanian suzerainty in 1561 and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1795. Predominantly Lutheran, it has formed part of Latvia since the country's independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/courland.jpg" alt="Courland" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edgars Šulcs · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ventspils_viduslaiku_pils_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Courland, in Latvian Kurzeme, is one of the Historical Latvian Lands. It lies in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, bordered on the north by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-riga">Gulf of Riga</a>, on the west by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> and on the south by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and its name means Land of Curonians. The landscape is low and undulating, with marshy coastlands and, inland, wooded dunes of pine, spruce, birch and oak, swamps and lakes. Its largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liepaja">Liepāja</a>. Of nearly a hundred rivers, only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lielupe">Lielupe</a> and the Venta are navigable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Curonians were a Baltic tribe with a strong warrior culture and a reputation for seafaring, living on the Baltic shore from before the 5th century. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-crusade">Livonian Crusades</a> they resisted German crusading orders for a long time. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitians">Samogitians</a> defeated the Livonian Order at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-durbe">Battle of Durbe</a> in 1260, uprisings followed, but in 1266 the whole of Courland was partitioned between the Livonian Order and the Archbishop of Riga. Curonian nobles in Kuldīga kept their personal freedom and some privileges.</p><p>From 1561 until 1795 the Duchy of Courland and Semigallia was a semi-independent state, nominally a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. The future Empress Anna served as regent from 1711, and after the ducal line died out in 1737 she arranged for the duchy to pass to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernst-johann-von-biron">Ernst Johann von Biron</a>. The duchy was one of the smallest European states to found overseas colonies, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunta-kinteh-island">James Island</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a>. In 1795 the last duke, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-von-biron">Peter von Biron</a>, ceded it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, and it became the Courland Governorate, where most land was held by nobles descended from German invaders and most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a> were landless labourers.</p><p>German forces took Courland in 1915. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> declared independence on 18 November 1918, Courland became one of its provinces. In 1944–1945 some 200,000 German troops were trapped in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland-pocket">Courland Pocket</a> until their surrender on 8 May 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Courland is part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> but is no longer an administrative unit; the Kurzeme Planning Region covers much of it. Its population has long been predominantly Lutheran, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, Catholic and Jewish communities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Courland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Latvian national consciousness had grown since the national awakening of the mid-nineteenth century, and in 1915 Latvian Riflemen units were formed in the Russian army to defend <b>Courland</b> and Riga against the Germans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Courland</b> was conquered by the Livonian Order in the 1250s and 1260s, the bishop received about a third of the land, scattered in several districts.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-courland">Bishopric of Courland</a></li><li><blockquote>It depended on supplies from <b>Courland</b> and on agreements with the kings of Kombo and Niumi.</blockquote><a href="/state/courland-gambia">Courland Gambia</a></li><li><blockquote>They landed at what became Great Courland Bay, built Fort Jacobus and the settlement of Jacobusstadt, and named the island New <b>Courland</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/courland-tobago">Courland Tobago</a></li><li><blockquote>The dukes of <b>Courland</b> repeatedly claimed the district and controlled it for periods, while the nobles resisted, preferring the distant Polish king.</blockquote><a href="/state/district-of-pilten">District of Pilten</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Livonian Order dissolved in 1561, its last master, Gotthard Kettler, secularised the Order's lands south of the Daugava and became hereditary Duke of <b>Courland</b> and Semigallia, a vassal of Poland-Lithuania.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia">Duchy of Courland and Semigallia</a></li></ul></section>
